WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.254
Judicial independence.

00:00:02.204 --> 00:00:05.102
Can courts actually check executive power?

00:00:05.102 --> 00:00:08.204
This page examines the documented record.

00:00:08.204 --> 00:00:13.795
The Federal Court ruled the Emergencies Act invocation was unreasonable and violated the Charter.

00:00:13.795 --> 00:00:20.829
That ruling came two years after the bank accounts were frozen, the protests were cleared, and the political objective was achieved.

00:00:20.829 --> 00:00:25.647
Vice Admiral Norman was prosecuted for three years before charges were stayed.

00:00:25.647 --> 00:00:31.784
The Ethics Commissioner found the Prime Minister violated the Conflict of Interest Act in the SNC-Lavalin affair.

00:00:31.784 --> 00:00:34.056
The consequence was zero.

00:00:34.056 --> 00:00:38.068
In each case, the legal system eventually reached the right conclusion.

00:00:38.068 --> 00:00:42.284
In each case, the remedy arrived too late to function as accountability.

00:00:42.284 --> 00:00:49.193
The executive achieved its objective, the court ruled after the fact, and no one was held accountable for the original action.

00:00:49.193 --> 00:00:50.988
The delay pattern.

00:00:50.988 --> 00:00:56.863
Every major judicial review of executive overreach in recent Canadian history follows the same timeline.

00:00:56.863 --> 00:00:58.909
The executive acts.

00:00:58.909 --> 00:01:01.431
Citizens are affected immediately.

00:01:01.431 --> 00:01:03.749
The legal challenge is filed.

00:01:03.749 --> 00:01:06.215
The court process takes years.

00:01:06.215 --> 00:01:10.090
The ruling arrives after the executive has achieved its objective.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:15.659
The remedy is declaratory — meaning the court says it was wrong, but cannot undo what was done.

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:18.909
This is not a failure of judicial independence.

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:22.204
Canadian courts are institutionally independent.

00:01:22.204 --> 00:01:25.204
This is a failure of judicial effectiveness.

00:01:25.204 --> 00:01:29.613
The remedy arrives too late to function as a constraint on executive power.

00:01:29.613 --> 00:01:31.374
The delay table.

00:01:31.374 --> 00:01:43.738
Emergencies Act: executive action February 2022, bank accounts frozen, Federal Court ruling January 2024, two years later, declared unreasonable, no accounts unfrozen retroactively.

00:01:43.738 --> 00:01:53.999
Norman prosecution: charges laid 2018, charges stayed May 2019, three years of career destruction, zero accountability for the prosecution.

00:01:53.999 --> 00:02:05.340
SNC-Lavalin: PMO interference 2018-2019, Ethics Commissioner finding August 2019, section 9 violation confirmed, zero consequences.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:13.613
Firearms OIC: executive ban May 2020, legal challenges filed immediately, litigation still ongoing six years later.

00:02:13.613 --> 00:02:15.386
Case analysis.

00:02:15.386 --> 00:02:18.613
Three landmark cases that demonstrate the pattern.

00:02:18.613 --> 00:02:20.670
Structural analysis.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.795
Why courts cannot effectively check executive power in real time.

00:02:24.795 --> 00:02:27.034
Three structural barriers.

00:02:27.034 --> 00:02:29.136
First: the timeline.

00:02:29.136 --> 00:02:31.579
Judicial review takes years.

00:02:31.579 --> 00:02:34.159
Executive action takes days.

00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:38.272
By the time the court rules, the executive has achieved its objective.

00:02:38.272 --> 00:02:41.136
Second: remedies are declaratory.

00:02:41.136 --> 00:02:43.443
Courts can say it was wrong.

00:02:43.443 --> 00:02:46.056
Courts cannot undo what was done.

00:02:46.056 --> 00:02:50.749
Frozen bank accounts cannot be retroactively unfrozen in a meaningful sense.

00:02:50.749 --> 00:02:54.499
Destroyed careers cannot be rebuilt by staying charges.

00:02:54.499 --> 00:02:56.886
Third: the cost asymmetry.

00:02:56.886 --> 00:03:00.295
The government litigates with unlimited public funds.

00:03:00.295 --> 00:03:03.147
Citizens litigate with personal savings.

00:03:03.147 --> 00:03:07.181
The Norman case cost over two million dollars in legal fees.

00:03:07.181 --> 00:03:11.579
Most citizens cannot afford to challenge executive overreach in court.

00:03:11.579 --> 00:03:13.624
The accountability gap.

00:03:13.624 --> 00:03:16.454
Courts are institutionally independent.

00:03:16.454 --> 00:03:19.488
Judges are appointed with security of tenure.

00:03:19.488 --> 00:03:23.352
The Federal Court rules against the government when the law requires it.

00:03:23.352 --> 00:03:26.602
This is not a failure of judicial independence.

00:03:26.602 --> 00:03:31.136
It is a failure of judicial effectiveness as a constraint on executive power.

00:03:31.136 --> 00:03:33.181
The executive acts.

00:03:33.181 --> 00:03:35.170
Citizens are harmed.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.874
Years pass.

00:03:36.874 --> 00:03:39.181
The court rules it was wrong.

00:03:39.181 --> 00:03:41.431
No one is held accountable.

00:03:41.431 --> 00:03:43.704
The harm is not undone.

00:03:43.704 --> 00:03:51.988
And the executive has learned that the cost of overreach is a future declaratory ruling — which carries no penalty and arrives after the objective is achieved.

00:03:51.988 --> 00:04:00.772
This is why the accountability scorecard shows zero enforcement: every oversight body can investigate, but none can constrain executive power in real time.
